        <h2 id="tips">Making It a Habit</h2>
        <p>
          The details that decide whether this becomes a weekly ritual or a
          one-off you forget:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Both of you add entries.</strong> Spend five minutes adding
            options together before the first spin.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Save and reuse your wheels.</strong> A bookmarked wheel
            means the next date night starts with a spin, not a debate, and you
            can refine the entries over time.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Set the veto rule before spinning.</strong> Agreeing after
            you see the result only reopens the argument.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Remove used entries for ordered decisions.</strong> If you
            are deciding who plans the next three dates, take each result off
            the wheel so everyone gets a turn.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Keep one "decide together" wildcard.</strong> It leaves a
            little room for spontaneity inside the system.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <div class="callout callout-tip">
          <div class="callout-title">🔬 Worth knowing</div>
          If you wonder whether anyone can steer the result, read our
          explanation of
          <a href="/blogs/is-spin-wheel-truly-random">whether a digital spin wheel is truly random</a>.
          To see how a wheel compares with other pickers, see
          <a href="/blogs/spin-wheel-vs-other-random-pickers">spin the wheel vs. other random pickers</a>.
        </div>

